# Copy to .env.local and fill in what you have. Every one of these is optional:
# the app runs without any of them, it just does less.

# Real songs: sung, produced, made by a music model rather than sketched in the
# browser. Get it from elevenlabs.io. A paid plan carries a commercial licence;
# the free tier does not, so tracks made on it cannot be released.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# The copilot, the writing help, song ideas, the trends scoring, and the help
# desk at /help. Without it those screens say so instead of pretending — and
# /help still works, because the form that reaches a person does not need a
# model.
#
# The two routes that call it without asking who is calling — the copilot and
# the help desk — are capped per address in `app/lib/server/brake.ts`, so a
# loop cannot spend the month's budget in an afternoon.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Accounts, and your songs following you between devices. Without these two the
# app signs you in on this device only and keeps your tracks in this browser.
# Set them up by running supabase/schema.sql in your project's SQL editor.
# The anon key is designed to be public — the policies in that file, not the
# key, decide what anyone can read or write. Never put the service_role key here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The service-role key, from the same Settings -> API page. This one bypasses
# every row-level policy, which is exactly why the server needs it: it writes
# the generation and purchase rows that must not be writable by the person they
# bill. It has no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on purpose — that prefix would ship it to
# every visitor's browser and hand them the whole database.
# Without it, usage is not metered and the free tier is effectively unlimited.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Taking payments. From dashboard.paystack.com -> Settings -> API Keys.
# Use the test key (sk_test_...) until you have run a payment end to end.
# Without it nothing can be charged, and the app says so rather than pretending
# a checkout exists.
PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=

# The three Paystack plans, so a membership renews every month instead of
# being a single charge. Create them once with:
#
#   PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxx node scripts/paystack-plans.mjs
#
# and paste the three codes it prints here. Without them the app still sells
# plans — as one month at a time, which is what it did before subscriptions.
# Test-mode plans only work with test-mode payments, so swap these when you
# swap the key above for a live one.
PAYSTACK_PLAN_MAKER=
PAYSTACK_PLAN_STUDIO=
PAYSTACK_PLAN_LABEL=

# Accounts that always have full access, whatever the memberships table says.
# For you, and for anyone demonstrating the app: testing the paid product should
# not need a hand-written SQL insert every time, and a demo that hits a free-tier
# wall in front of an audience is a bad demo.
# Comma-separated. Matched on the verified email from the sign-in token, never on
# anything the request claims about itself. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so the list
# never reaches a browser.
OWNER_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The welcome video on the landing page
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each language plays its own recording, or nothing. A language with no
# recording set shows no player at all, rather than the other language's.
# English falls back to /welcome.mp4 in public/ when unset; Afrikaans has no
# fallback, so leaving it blank hides the player on the Afrikaans page.
# These carry the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix on purpose: they are public addresses for
# public files, and the browser is what needs them.
#
# WHERE THE FILE LIVES MATTERS. The app serves a Content-Security-Policy, and a
# video on an origin that policy does not list is blocked by us — the player
# stays black, nothing on screen says why, and the only trace is a line in the
# browser console. `/welcome.mp4` in public/ and Vercel Blob
# (*.public.blob.vercel-storage.com) are both already allowed. Anywhere else,
# add the origin to `media-src` in next.config.mjs at the same time, and to
# `img-src` for the cover frames below.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WELCOME_VIDEO_ENGLISH=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WELCOME_VIDEO_AFRIKAANS=

# The cover frame shown before anybody presses play, per language. Optional:
# without one the player is a flat rectangle until the browser has decoded
# enough of the video, which reads as a thing that failed to load. COVERE is
# the English cover, COVERA the Afrikaans one. A cover is never used for the
# other language — an Afrikaans title card over an English recording is worse
# than no card.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WELCOME_VIDEO_COVERE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_WELCOME_VIDEO_COVERA=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where this app lives
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The address printed to people — the studio header shows a creator
# "<host>/@their-handle" as their own, and the terms and privacy notice give
# the contact address. Unset it falls back to the Vercel address, which is the
# one we actually have.
#
# futurebox.app is NOT ours: DNS resolves it to somebody else's server, and
# eight of the ten obvious FutureBox domains are taken. See docs/GOING_LIVE.md.
# Set this once the domain is settled, and redeploy — NEXT_PUBLIC_ values are
# baked in at build time, so saving the variable alone changes nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_HOST=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email: receipts, the welcome, the cancellation, and warnings to you
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING MAIL_FROM. It is the part people get wrong.
#
# MAIL_FROM has to be an address on a domain whose DNS you control, with the
# SPF and DKIM records your provider gives you. It CANNOT be a free mailbox.
# Gmail publishes a DMARC policy telling every receiving server to reject mail
# claiming to be from gmail.com that did not come from Google — so sending
# "from" futureboxapp@gmail.com through any provider means receipts land in
# spam or are refused outright.
#
# That means this needs the domain settled first. See docs/GOING_LIVE.md §2,
# which is still open. Until MAIL_FROM is set, nothing is sent and every room
# keeps working — a missing receipt never blocks a payment.
#
# MAIL_REPLY_TO is different and has no such constraint: it is where a reply
# goes, and any mailbox works. It defaults to futureboxapp@gmail.com, so
# somebody hitting reply on a receipt reaches you.
MAIL_API_KEY=
MAIL_FROM=
MAIL_REPLY_TO=futureboxapp@gmail.com
# Only if you use something other than Resend with the same request shape.
MAIL_API_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The watch: telling you when something needs you
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /api/watch reads the ElevenLabs allowance and emails OWNER_EMAIL when it is
# three quarters, nine tenths and nearly gone — once each per billing month, so
# a warning stays a warning rather than becoming noise you filter.
#
# It reports the size of your bill and it can send email, so it refuses without
# a secret rather than defaulting to open. Set both of these to the same value:
# WATCH_SECRET is what the route checks, CRON_SECRET is what Vercel's scheduler
# sends. vercel.json runs it daily at 07:00 UTC.
WATCH_SECRET=
CRON_SECRET=
